---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { PHONE, PHONE_TEL, SITE_NAME } from '../../lib/site.ts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Scope & disclosure"
  description="What Foundation Repair Advocate provides and what we do not — informational floor surveys and experience-based guidance, not engineering stamps or repair contracts."
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1 class="page-title">Scope &amp; disclosure</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Please read this before we schedule a visit. It protects everyone and keeps expectations
      honest.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>What this service is</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE_NAME} provides independent floor elevation surveys, on-site observation, and
        plain-language discussion based on field experience and forensic techniques used in
        foundation repair and related geotechnical work across Arizona, New Mexico, Colorado,
        Texas, and Utah.
      </p>
      <p>
        We provide measured documentation and an independent practical opinion. We do not diagnose
        structural adequacy, design repairs, or provide stamped engineering opinions.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a <strong>non-destructive first look with known limits</strong>: measure what the
        floors are doing, talk through what that may mean, and help you decide whether monitoring,
        drainage, repair, or a full engineering investigation is warranted — without fear and
        without a pier to sell.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>First step — not a full investigation</h2>
      <p>
        A complete picture of a foundation problem can involve geotechnical exploration (borings,
        soil labs) and/or stamped structural engineering. Those services look at factors we cannot
        see from the living room — conditions in the dirt, design loads, and formal repair design.
        They also cost significantly more and often take longer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our visit does not replace that work. It often comes <em>before</em> it. Many homes never
        need the full package. Some do — and then you hire the right PE or geotech firm with better
        questions and a measured baseline already in hand.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Non-destructive first look with known limits.</strong> Floor survey and
          observation first. Full geotechnical or structural investigation when the facts call for
          it — at that higher price, for that deeper scope.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What this service is not</h2>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Not a licensed Arizona professional engineering (PE) service.</strong> We do not
          provide stamped engineering reports, structural calculations, or designs intended for
          permitting or construction documents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a home inspection</strong> under Arizona home inspector licensing. We do not
          perform a general home inspection or certify a property for a real estate transaction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a foundation repair contractor.</strong> We do not sell, install, or bid
          helical piers, push piers, foam, or other structural repair. We have no commission on
          repair work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a subsurface investigation.</strong> We cannot see into the dirt. Soil
          conditions below grade, moisture at depth, and hidden defects require geotechnical
          exploration, excavation, or other methods we do not perform on a standard survey visit.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What we can and cannot know</h2>
      <p>
        Floor elevations, visible cracks, door and window behavior, drainage at the surface, and
        patterns we have seen over years of field work are useful. They are still
        <em>observations and experience</em> — not proof of what is happening below the slab or
        footing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two homes with similar floor maps can have different causes. Two cracks that look the same
        can mean different things. We will tell you when the picture is clear enough to act, when
        monitoring is wiser, and when a licensed engineer or geotechnical firm is the right next
        step.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>You are hiring judgment and measurement — not a guarantee.</strong> Measured
          documentation and practical opinion only — not structural adequacy, repair design, or
          stamped engineering. Our written summary and conversation are informational. They do not
          replace engineering, legal advice, contractor bids, or your own decision-making.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Real estate transactions</h2>
      <p>
        Agents and parties often need a calm first look when cracks or settlement concerns show up
        mid-transaction. Our survey and notes are informational — based on experience and forensic
        observation — so everyone can talk about facts before panic or a repair bid drives the deal.
      </p>
      <p>
        They are not a certification that the foundation is sound, not a seller disclosure document,
        and not a substitute for inspections, lender requirements, or a full geotechnical / structural
        investigation when one is required. Contract language and disclosure remain the
        responsibility of the parties and their advisors.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Acknowledgement (for clients)</h2>
      <p>
        By scheduling and completing a visit with {SITE_NAME}, you acknowledge that:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>You understand the scope and limits above.</li>
        <li>
          Findings are based on accessible observation, floor elevation data, and professional
          experience — not subsurface exploration or stamped engineering.
        </li>
        <li>
          Recommendations are guidance for education and decision-making, not a directive to repair
          or not repair.
        </li>
        <li>
          You will seek a licensed Arizona PE, geotechnical firm, attorney, or contractor when the
          situation requires their role — and that we will say so when we believe that is needed.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--muted);">
        A signed copy can be included with your engagement paperwork when we schedule. This page is
        the public statement of the same limits.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        If any of this is unclear before you book, ask. We would rather talk it through than leave
        an assumption on either side.
      </p>
      <div class="contact-box">
        <a class="phone-link" href={`tel:${PHONE_TEL}`}>{PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
